# ============================================================================
#  THE FLOOR — what the village has already managed, and may not lose.
#
#  Read by tools/Noir.Sim/Ratio.cs and asserted by tools/Noir.Core.Tests/
#  TwoToOneTests.cs. Every line is a one-way ratchet: raised by hand, in a
#  commit that says why, and never lowered — with exactly one exception.
#
#  THE ONE EXCEPTION, AND IT IS THE WHOLE ANTI-MECHANISATION CLAUSE:
#
#      ratio.median may be lowered ONLY in the same commit that RAISES
#      texture.median.
#
#  You may trade legibility for texture. You may not lose both. Without that
#  rule the cheapest possible route to a passing ratio is to strip the village
#  of the things that make it a village — errands are where standing in a
#  line, carrying something and going somewhere new all come from, so
#  DayPlan.cs's second errand could be deleted and the number would IMPROVE
#  while the place died. texture.median is what catches that, and it catches
#  it immediately.
#
#  ---- when these are void ---------------------------------------------------
#
#  Salience.DaysWatched is fourteen. The ratio is a function of the watch
#  length — measured at 3 / 7 / 14 days it falls — so "2:1" means "2:1 at
#  exactly fourteen days". If anybody ever changes that constant, EVERY LINE
#  BELOW IS VOID and must be re-measured in the same commit. The same goes for
#  Salience.Confirmations, the day-class split, and Eyewitness.SightRange.
#
#  ---- how these were measured -----------------------------------------------
#
#  `dotnet run --project Noir.Sim -- ratio --seeds 1979,1980,1981`, fourteen
#  days, Release. Each line is the WORST of the three villages, rounded down —
#  a floor that only one seed clears is not a floor.
#
#      seed 1979   112 people   median 1.10   p10 0.37   tex 3/22   sight 5.5%
#      seed 1980   106 people   median 1.06   p10 0.33   tex 2/22   sight 5.6%
#      seed 1981   104 people   median 1.08   p10 0.30   tex 4/21   sight 5.4%
#
#  The village is at about 1.1 : 1 and the rule is 2 : 1. It fails, on three
#  of the six gates, and it is meant to: this file records where it started
#  from, not where it is going.
# ============================================================================

#  ---- 2026-07-28: RE-MEASURED. The eye's vocabulary changed. -----------------
#
#  Two things landed together, and the floors above were void the moment the
#  second one did.
#
#  1. DayPlanner.ChooseErrand gained a dwelling. People call on their neighbours
#     now. Before it, this instrument watched 112 people for a fortnight and
#     recorded ZERO visits in 1,568 household-days — the errand list was a list
#     of AMENITIES, and a neighbour is not an amenity. "Whether anybody would
#     notice her gone" used to confirm for EVERY VILLAGER because it was true of
#     every one of them; it now confirms 185 times instead of 336.
#
#  2. ObservedAct gained CalledOnSomebody. Going up a path that is not your own
#     is a different kind of moment from going home, and a watcher opposite can
#     see the difference. WHOSE path is not recorded and never can be — that
#     would be an identity, and this assembly is built so it cannot hold one.
#
#  The second is why every line below is re-measured rather than argued over.
#  The header says these are void if Salience.Confirmations, the day-class split
#  or Eyewitness.SightRange move; adding a verb is the same category, because a
#  measurement taken with a smaller vocabulary is not comparable to one taken
#  with a larger.
#
#  ---- and the thing this file caught, which is why it exists ----------------
#
#  On seed 1979 the change reads as a clear win: median 1.10 -> 1.20, p10
#  0.37 -> 0.44, and the sign inversion closing, 0.86 -> 1.01, so a fuller life
#  no longer scores worse than an emptier one.
#
#  Across all three it is roughly NEUTRAL. Worst-of-three median is 1.06, which
#  is exactly where it was. Seed 1981's median villager lost a kind of moment,
#  21 -> 20, because a visit SUBSTITUTES for an errand — the person who would
#  have walked to the green is now in somebody's kitchen — and calling on a
#  neighbour is a shorter walk than the errand it replaced.
#
#  Read as a single-seed result it looked like progress. It was not. That is the
#  whole argument for a three-seed floor, and it is recorded here rather than
#  quietly smoothed away, because the next person to move this number will be
#  tempted by exactly the same reading.
#
#      seed 1979   112 people   median 1.20   p10 0.44   tex 4/22   sight 4.8%
#      seed 1980   106 people   median 1.08   p10 0.39   tex 2/21   sight 5.0%
#      seed 1981   104 people   median 1.06   p10 0.37   tex 4/20   sight 5.0%
#
#  Still 1.1 : 1 against a rule of 2 : 1, and still failing on three of six.
#  The instrument's own verdict has not changed and should be believed: the fix
#  is MORE KINDS OF MOMENT. Visiting added a verb and took an errand away, so it
#  very nearly paid for itself and no more. What has not been tried yet is the
#  one the report keeps naming — Citizen.Particulars is read by two inspectors
#  and by NO line of simulation code. Nine hundred clauses of texture exist and
#  not one of them is ever ENACTED.
# ============================================================================

#  ---- 2026-07-28, later: THE DINNER HOUR. The exception clause, invoked. ----
#
#  DayPlanner now cuts a 45-minute dinner break out of any shift or school day
#  that straddles midday, and the break is spent OUT — the green, the
#  churchyard, the playground, the pub. It is the first change made because the
#  `street` instrument asked for it: the village measured 0.2 of 112 people
#  visible at half past twelve against 13.6 at half past eight, because a shift
#  was one unbroken block and thirty-seven adults and twenty children were
#  sealed indoors through the best light of the day.
#
#  THIS LOWERS ratio.median AND THAT IS THE EXCEPTION THIS FILE ALLOWS. Stated
#  plainly, as the rule requires:
#
#      ratio.median   1.06  -> 1.04   (worst of three, down)
#      texture.median   20  ->   23   (worst of three, up)
#      texture.min       2  ->    6   (worst of three, tripled)
#      ratio.p10      0.37  -> 0.375  (up, floor unchanged after rounding)
#      sight.median  0.048  -> 0.052  (up)
#
#  The trade, honestly: a person who goes out at the same time every weekday is
#  more legible, and that is a real cost — the dinner hour is a habit, and this
#  game is eventually about habits being read. What it buys is variety. The
#  median villager gained three kinds of moment and the WORST-SERVED villager
#  went from two kinds in a fortnight to six. That is the number this file
#  exists to protect, and it moved further than the one it cost.
#
#      seed 1979   112 people   median 1.21   p10 0.44   tex 9/23   sight 5.2%
#      seed 1980   106 people   median 1.05   p10 0.40   tex 10/23  sight 5.7%
#      seed 1981   104 people   median 1.05   p10 0.38   tex 6/23   sight 5.5%
#
#  Note seed 1979 alone reads as a straight win — median UP to 1.21 and texture
#  min 4 -> 9, clearing the G3 gate outright. It is 1980 and 1981 that pay for
#  it. The file warned the last person about exactly this reading and it caught
#  the next one too, which is me.
#
#  What did NOT improve: the sign inversion reopened, 1.01 -> 0.94 on 1979. A
#  fuller life scores worse again, because the dinner hour puts people outdoors
#  ALONE and time outdoors alone still accrues tactical facts faster than
#  texture. That is the same finding as before and it has the same fix, which
#  still has not been tried: ENACT THE PARTICULARS. Nine hundred clauses, read
#  by two inspectors and by no line of simulation code.
# ============================================================================

#  ---- 2026-07-28, later still: THE EAST QUARTER AND THE WORKS ---------------
#
#  Two content changes, no instrument change, so these floors are comparable
#  with the ones above rather than void.
#
#  1. The map went 170 -> 210 wide and five terraces went up east of Mill Lane.
#     112 people -> 158. The old map was FULL: four terraces were tried inside
#     it first and the layout checker refused every one, because the only gaps
#     left were the green, the playground and the allotments.
#
#  2. Calloway & Sons, a factory employing 34 on two shifts. The estate had
#     arrived with no work attached to it and 59% of working-age adults had
#     nowhere to be; that is now 23.4%.
#
#  EVERY LINE IMPROVED. Worst of three seeds, old -> new:
#
#      ratio.median     1.04 -> 1.21
#      ratio.p10        0.37 -> 0.52
#      texture.median     23 ->   24
#      texture.min         6 ->    8
#      sight.median    0.052 -> 0.053
#
#  texture.min crossing 8 means G3 - "distinct texture, minimum >= 8" - now
#  PASSES on all three seeds. It has failed since the instrument was built. The
#  village has stopped containing anybody who yields fewer than eight kinds of
#  moment in a fortnight.
#
#  Worth recording WHY, because it was not the obvious cause. The population
#  going up does not by itself give any individual a more various life. What
#  did it was the WORK: a second large employer on split shifts means a
#  neighbour who leaves at seven and one who leaves at three, lights on in the
#  same terrace at different hours, and somebody walking home past you at an
#  hour when the street used to be empty. Employment is texture.
#
#  Still failing, and still the two that matter: median 1.21 against a rule of
#  2.00, and a tenth percentile of 0.52 against 1.00. The instrument's verdict
#  has not changed - the fix is MORE KINDS OF MOMENT, and the particulars are
#  still not enacted.
# ============================================================================

ratio.median      1.21
ratio.p10         0.52
texture.median      24
texture.min          8
sight.median     0.053
